course: dictionary
lesson: 1862
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word coil is a good example. «The snake coiled up under the rock.» Here coil is a verb: the snake wound itself into rings. «Mum put the hose in a neat coil.» Here coil is a noun: a set of rings.
explain: Look at the words around the word. In «a coil of rope», the rope is wound in rings, so coil must mean a set of loops. In «a coil of wire» that carries electricity, the clue is electricity, so coil means a wire wound in rings.
explain: A good check is to put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. «Shuffled off this mortal coil» is a joke, so it does not mean loops at all: it means died. Context tells you that, not the single word on its own.
fact: Coil can be a noun (a coil of rope) or a verb (to coil, the snake coiled up).
fact: «Coil» means a series of rings or loops made by winding something long.
fact: «Coil» can also mean a wire wound in rings that carries electricity, as in a heater or an electromagnet.
fact: «Shuffled off this mortal coil» is a joke from Shakespeare's Hamlet, and it means died.
fact: A good check is to swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The snake coiled up under the rock.» What does coiled mean here? | Ran straight out across the field | Fell off the rock | *Wound itself into rings | Shouted loudly at the rock
exercise: «Mum put the hose in a neat coil.» What is the coil here? | A kind of flower | A loud noise from the garden | A small wooden box | *A set of rings or loops of something long
exercise: «A coil next to a magnet pushes the sound out of a loudspeaker.» What is a coil in this sentence? | A pile of old sweaters | *A wire wound in rings that carries electricity | A tin of paint | A loud song
test: «The heater glows because electricity flows through its coil.» What does coil mean? | A snake that is sleeping | A pile of loose stones | A long walk in the hills | *A wire wound in rings that carries electricity
test: «The rope lay in a tidy coil on the deck.» Is coil a noun or a verb in this sentence? | *Noun | Adverb | Adjective | Verb
exam: The Mock Turtle's joke «Fainting in Coils» is a sound-alike for painting in oils; what does the joke rely on? | Rope that is tied in knots | A sum about money | *Words that sound alike but mean something different | A snake that faints
